findings.detail:
The initializer parsing added here stores init declarations as MemberKind::Method. For the ordinary input `class Pet {
    init() {}
    func speak() -> String { return "base" }
}
class Dog: Pet {}
`, calling replace_inheritance_impl for Dog with methods_opt=None (or Some(&[])) now includes init in methods_to_forward because Dog has no explicit initializer and the filter excludes only constructor plus C++ constructors/destructors. transform_swift then passes Pet's `init() {}` declaration to swift_forwarding_method, which immediately returns `Swift base method has no `func` declaration`. Before this change, the parser omitted initializers, so the same default path forwarded speak and generated Dog's delegate-initializing init. Retain initializer discovery for stripping override, but do not treat it as a forwardable method. This refusal removes previously useful ordinary behavior, rather than rejecting an opaque declaration.
